.TH "frontend/node_modules/js-tokens Directory Reference" 3 "Version 0.1" "Chat-ME!" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/js-tokens Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
A regex that tokenizes JavaScript\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
var jsTokens = require("js\-tokens")\&.default

var jsString = "var foo=opts\&.foo;\\n\&.\&.\&."

jsString\&.match(jsTokens)
// ["var", " ", "foo", "=", "opts", "\&.", "foo", ";", "\\n", \&.\&.\&.]
.fi
.PP
.SH "Installation"
.PP
\fRnpm install js-tokens\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
import jsTokens from "js\-tokens"
// or:
var jsTokens = require("js\-tokens")\&.default
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.SS "\fRjsTokens\fP"
A regex with the \fRg\fP flag that matches JavaScript tokens\&.

.PP
The regex \fIalways\fP matches, even invalid JavaScript and the empty string\&.

.PP
The next match is always directly after the previous\&.
.SS "\fRvar token = matchToToken(match)\fP"
.PP
.nf
import {matchToToken} from "js\-tokens"
// or:
var matchToToken = require("js\-tokens")\&.matchToToken
.fi
.PP

.PP
Takes a \fRmatch\fP returned by \fRjsTokens\&.exec(string)\fP, and returns a \fR{type: String, value: String}\fP object\&. The following types are available:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
string
.IP "\(bu" 2
comment
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex
.IP "\(bu" 2
number
.IP "\(bu" 2
name
.IP "\(bu" 2
punctuator
.IP "\(bu" 2
whitespace
.IP "\(bu" 2
invalid
.PP

.PP
Multi-line comments and strings also have a \fRclosed\fP property indicating if the token was closed or not (see below)\&.

.PP
Comments and strings both come in several flavors\&. To distinguish them, check if the token starts with \fR//\fP, \fR/*\fP, \fR'\fP, \fR"\fP or \fR ` \fP\&.

.PP
Names are ECMAScript IdentifierNames, that is, including both identifiers and keywords\&. You may use \fRis-keyword-js\fP to tell them apart\&.

.PP
Whitespace includes both line terminators and other whitespace\&.
.SH "ECMAScript support"
.PP
The intention is to always support the latest ECMAScript version whose feature set has been finalized\&.

.PP
If adding support for a newer version requires changes, a new version with a major verion bump will be released\&.

.PP
Currently, ECMAScript 2018 is supported\&.
.SH "Invalid code handling"
.PP
Unterminated strings are still matched as strings\&. JavaScript strings cannot contain (unescaped) newlines, so unterminated strings simply end at the end of the line\&. Unterminated template strings can contain unescaped newlines, though, so they go on to the end of input\&.

.PP
Unterminated multi-line comments are also still matched as comments\&. They simply go on to the end of the input\&.

.PP
Unterminated regex literals are likely matched as division and whatever is inside the regex\&.

.PP
Invalid ASCII characters have their own capturing group\&.

.PP
Invalid non-ASCII characters are treated as names, to simplify the matching of names (except unicode spaces which are treated as whitespace)\&. Note: See also the \fBES2018\fP section\&.

.PP
Regex literals may contain invalid regex syntax\&. They are still matched as regex literals\&. They may also contain repeated regex flags, to keep the regex simple\&.

.PP
Strings may contain invalid escape sequences\&.
.SH "Limitations"
.PP
Tokenizing JavaScript using regexes—in fact, _one single regex_—won’t be perfect\&. But that’s not the point either\&.

.PP
You may compare jsTokens with \fResprima\fP by using \fResprima-compare\&.js\fP\&. See \fRnpm run esprima-compare\fP!
.SS "Template string interpolation"
Template strings are matched as single tokens, from the starting \fR ` \fP to the ending \fR ` \fP, including interpolations (whose tokens are not matched individually)\&.

.PP
Matching template string interpolations requires recursive balancing of \fR{\fP and \fR}\fP—something that JavaScript regexes cannot do\&. Only one level of nesting is supported\&.
.SS "Division and regex literals collision"
Consider this example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var g = 9\&.82
var number = bar / 2/g

var regex = / 2/g
.fi
.PP

.PP
A human can easily understand that in the \fRnumber\fP line we’re dealing with division, and in the \fRregex\fP line we’re dealing with a regex literal\&. How come? Because humans can look at the whole code to put the \fR/\fP characters in context\&. A JavaScript regex cannot\&. It only sees forwards\&. (Well, ES2018 regexes can also look backwards\&. See the \fBES2018\fP section)\&.

.PP
When the \fRjsTokens\fP regex scans throught the above, it will see the following at the end of both the \fRnumber\fP and \fRregex\fP rows:

.PP
.PP
.nf
/ 2/g
.fi
.PP

.PP
It is then impossible to know if that is a regex literal, or part of an expression dealing with division\&.

.PP
Here is a similar case:

.PP
.PP
.nf
foo /= 2/g
foo(/= 2/g)
.fi
.PP

.PP
The first line divides the \fRfoo\fP variable with \fR2/g\fP\&. The second line calls the \fRfoo\fP function with the regex literal \fR/= 2/g\fP\&. Again, since \fRjsTokens\fP only sees forwards, it cannot tell the two cases apart\&.

.PP
There are some cases where we \fIcan\fP tell division and regex literals apart, though\&.

.PP
First off, we have the simple cases where there’s only one slash in the line:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var foo = 2/g
foo /= 2
.fi
.PP

.PP
Regex literals cannot contain newlines, so the above cases are correctly identified as division\&. Things are only problematic when there are more than one non-comment slash in a single line\&.

.PP
Secondly, not every character is a valid regex flag\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
var number = bar / 2/e
.fi
.PP

.PP
The above example is also correctly identified as division, because \fRe\fP is not a valid regex flag\&. I initially wanted to future-proof by allowing \fR[a-zA-Z]*\fP (any letter) as flags, but it is not worth it since it increases the amount of ambigous cases\&. So only the standard \fRg\fP, \fRm\fP, \fRi\fP, \fRy\fP and \fRu\fP flags are allowed\&. This means that the above example will be identified as division as long as you don’t rename the \fRe\fP variable to some permutation of \fRgmiyus\fP 1 to 6 characters long\&.

.PP
Lastly, we can look \fIforward\fP for information\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
If the token following what looks like a regex literal is not valid after a regex literal, but is valid in a division expression, then the regex literal is treated as division instead\&. For example, a flagless regex cannot be followed by a string, number or name, but all of those three can be the denominator of a division\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Generally, if what looks like a regex literal is followed by an operator, the regex literal is treated as division instead\&. This is because regexes are seldomly used with operators (such as \fR+\fP, \fR*\fP, \fR&&\fP and \fR==\fP), but division could likely be part of such an expression\&.
.PP

.PP
Please consult the regex source and the test cases for precise information on when regex or division is matched (should you need to know)\&. In short, you could sum it up as:

.PP
If the end of a statement looks like a regex literal (even if it isn’t), it will be treated as one\&. Otherwise it should work as expected (if you write sane code)\&.
.SS "ES2018"
ES2018 added some nice regex improvements to the language\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRUnicode property escapes\fP should allow telling names and invalid non-ASCII characters apart without blowing up the regex size\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRLookbehind assertions\fP should allow matching telling division and regex literals apart in more cases\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRNamed capture groups\fP might simplify some things\&.
.PP

.PP
These things would be nice to do, but are not critical\&. They probably have to wait until the oldest maintained Node\&.js LTS release supports those features\&.
